 ### Bilateral gradient filtering for meshes
-This is the core filtering mechanism used in the method. It replaces the postfilter on $m_t$ with a cross-bilateral filter using vertex normals $\hat{n}$ for the data term. It samples $K=32$ directions $s_i \in S^2$ on the unit sphere using the Sobol sequence. It computes the filter efficiently by concatenating vectors $W(\hat{n}, s_i)h$ and $W(\hat{n}, s_i)\mathbf{1}$ into matrix $M = [W(\hat{n}, s_1)h \dots W(\hat{n}, s_K)h \mid W(\hat{n}, s_1)\mathbf{1} \dots W(\hat{n}, s_K)\mathbf{1}]$, then computing $(I + \lambda L)^{-1} M$ using a single matrix solve. The filtered result is $\tilde{h} = \frac{\sum_{i=1}^K \Phi(\hat{n}, s_i) (I+\lambda L)^{-1} W(\hat{n}, s_i) h}{\sum_{i=1}^K \Phi(\hat{n}, s_i) (I+\lambda L)^{-1} W(\hat{n}, s_i) \mathbf{1}}$. The diagonal elements of $W_i$ are computed using the von Mises-Fisher kernel $\exp(\hat{n} \cdot s_i / \sigma_d)$, and $\Phi_i$ using $\exp(\hat{n} \cdot s_i / \sigma_\phi)$ [S1].
